import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Callout
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../../Ui/TableWrap

## The email package: installing the plugin, sending a message from a job, several recipients and
## the rest of a message, the SMTP server's URL, the dev mailbox, and what it can't do yet. Static: the samples are server/test/app's, which the
## server's tests run.
meta = {
	title: "Email",
	description: "packages/email, a plugin for Roc Solid's server: email over SMTP, as text or text and HTML, to several recipients with Cc, Bcc, Reply-To and headers of your own, sent from a job so a failed send is tried again; with no SMTP server, kept in memory and shown in the dev mailbox.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "install", label: "Installing it" },
		{ id: "send", label: "Sending an email" },
		{ id: "recipients", label: "Several recipients, Cc, Bcc, Reply-To" },
		{ id: "smtp", label: "Connecting to an SMTP server" },
		{ id: "mailbox", label: "The dev mailbox" },
		{ id: "inside", label: "How it works" },
		{ id: "limits", label: "Not yet" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="The ecosystem · email"
				title="Email that goes out over SMTP, or waits in a mailbox while you develop"
			>
				A welcome, a sign-in link, a receipt: <code>Email.send!</code> hands a message to your SMTP server,
				as text or as text and HTML. While you develop you don't set an SMTP server, so the message is
				kept in the server's memory instead, and the dev mailbox shows it. Nothing reaches a real inbox by
				accident, and nothing is lost by accident either: a deployed server with no SMTP server doesn't
				start.
			</PageHero>
			<p>
				The package runs on the server only. It is a <a href={"${Paths.ecosystem}#plugins"}>plugin</a>: the
				plugin adds the dev mailbox's pages and checks your SMTP server when the app starts. Sending works
				through the <code>Email</code> module.
			</p>

			<Heading id="install">Installing it</Heading>
			<p>
				Name the package in your app's header (<code>{"email: \"../packages/email/main.roc\""}</code>), import
				<code>email.Email</code>, and list its plugin in your program's <code>plugins</code>:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.email_plugin.file}, in part"} source={Sources.email_plugin.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>Both of the plugin's options have defaults:</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Default</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>from : Str</code></td><td><code>MAIL_FROM</code> from the environment, or else <code>{"rocx <rocx@localhost>"}</code></td><td>who email is from: <code>{"\"Acme <hello@example.com>\""}</code>, unless <code>MAIL_FROM</code> is set, which wins, so a deploy names the sender</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"smtp_url! : {} => Str"}</code></td><td>reads <code>SMTP_URL</code> from the environment</td><td>where email goes, found once when the server starts; give your own function to read it from somewhere else, such as a secrets vault. <code>""</code> means no SMTP server, which only <code>--dev</code> (and <code>--debug</code>, for tests) allows.</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>

			<Heading id="send">Sending an email</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>{"Email.send!({ to, subject, text, html })"}</code> sends one message and waits for the SMTP
				server's answer. <code>to</code> is an address, with or without a name
				(<code>{"\"Ada Lovelace <ada@example.com>\""}</code>), or several (below). <code>html</code> may be
				left out, or <code>""</code>, for a text-only email. It answers <code>{"Ok({})"}</code>, or
				<code>EmailErr</code> with the reason: an address it can't read, a header it refuses, a server that
				refuses the message or can't be reached, or no SMTP server outside <code>--dev</code>.
			</p>
			<p>
				Send from a <a href={Paths.ecosystem_jobs}>job</a> rather than from the request itself. The request
				that queues the job doesn't wait for the SMTP server, and a send that fails is tried again later.
				Here is a job that sends, from the server platform's test app:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_mail_job.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_mail_job.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				The job returns the send's error as its own, so the jobs plugin tries it again, a little later each
				time.
			</p>
			<Callout kind="note" title="No SMTP server: the mailbox under --dev, a refusal anywhere else">
				<p>
					Under <code>--dev</code> (as <code>rocx dev</code> starts the server) with no SMTP server,
					<code>Email.send!</code> answers <code>{"Ok({})"}</code> and keeps the message for the dev mailbox,
					with a line on the log (<code>email: kept, not sent (no SMTP_URL)</code>). So does
					<code>--debug</code>, which tests start the server with. Anywhere else the message would be lost,
					so a server that lists the plugin and has no SMTP server doesn't start: it says
					<code>{"email: SMTP_URL isn't set, so email can't be sent: …"}</code> and exits. Without the plugin,
					each <code>Email.send!</code> answers <code>EmailErr</code> saying the same.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<Heading id="recipients">Several recipients, Cc, Bcc, Reply-To</Heading>
			<p>
				A message is a record with defaults (<code>Email.Message</code>): only <code>to</code>,
				<code>subject</code> and <code>text</code> must be given, and the rest are there when you need them:
			</p>
			<Code
				title="Minutes, to the team"
				source={"Email.send!({\n\tto: \"Ada <ada@example.com>, \\\"Lovelace, Grace\\\" <grace@example.com>\",\n\tcc: [\"lin@example.com\"],\n\tbcc: [\"archive@example.com\"],\n\treply_to: \"team@example.com\",\n\theaders: [(\"List-Unsubscribe\", \"<https://example.com/unsubscribe?t=\${token}>\")],\n\tsubject: \"Minutes\",\n\ttext: minutes,\n})"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Default</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>to : Str</code></td><td>(must be given)</td><td>one address or several, separated by commas, as an email's <code>To</code> header has them; a comma inside a quoted name is the name's (<code>{"\"Lovelace, Ada\" <ada@example.com>"}</code>). At least one.</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>html : Str</code></td><td><code>""</code></td><td>the same as HTML; <code>""</code> for text alone</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>cc : List(Str)</code></td><td><code>[]</code></td><td>more who see it, in its <code>Cc</code> header; each item one address or several</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>bcc : List(Str)</code></td><td><code>[]</code></td><td>more who get it, named in no header: only the SMTP server is told, so nobody who gets it sees them</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>reply_to : Str</code></td><td><code>""</code></td><td>where a reply goes; <code>""</code>: to who it is from</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"headers : List((Str, Str))"}</code></td><td><code>[]</code></td><td>headers of your own, such as <code>List-Unsubscribe</code></td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				<code>to</code> stays a <code>Str</code>, so every message written for one recipient still is one.
				For a list of people, join it: <code>{"Str.join_with(addresses, \", \")"}</code>. The SMTP server is
				told every address in <code>to</code>, <code>cc</code> and <code>bcc</code>, each once. If it
				refuses one, nobody gets the message, and <code>Email.send!</code> says which address it refused.
			</p>
			<p>
				A header of your own can't break the message: one whose name or value has a line break in it
				(which would start a header of its own, a <code>Bcc</code> that quietly adds a recipient, say) is
				refused, and so are the headers the package writes itself (<code>From</code>, <code>To</code>,
				<code>Cc</code>, <code>Bcc</code>, <code>Reply-To</code>, <code>Subject</code>, <code>Date</code>,
				<code>Message-ID</code>, <code>MIME-Version</code>, <code>Content-*</code>): use the field instead.
				A value that isn't plain ASCII is encoded, as the subject is.
			</p>
			<Callout kind="note" title="A message you build first is an Email.Message">
				<p>
					A record written straight into <code>Email.send!</code>, or returned by a function whose type
					says <code>Email.Message</code>, gets the defaults. A function typed with a plain record
					(<code>{"-> { to : Str, subject : Str, text : Str, html : Str }"}</code>) or an untyped local
					doesn't: write <code>Email.Message</code> in its type.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<Heading id="smtp">Connecting to an SMTP server</Heading>
			<p><code>SMTP_URL</code> (or what your <code>smtp_url!</code> returns) says where to connect and how:</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>URL</th><th>Connection</th><th>Default port</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>smtps://user:password@host</code></td><td>TLS from the start</td><td>465</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>smtp://user:password@host?tls=required</code></td><td>STARTTLS; fails if the server doesn't offer it</td><td>587</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>smtp://user:password@host</code></td><td>STARTTLS, required as above: a password never goes in clear</td><td>25</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>smtp://host?tls=opportunistic</code></td><td>STARTTLS if the server offers it, plain otherwise (with a user: refused if it isn't offered)</td><td>25</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>smtp://host</code></td><td>plain, no TLS (a local relay, or a test server)</td><td>25</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>smtp://user:password@host?tls=never</code></td><td>plain, the password too (a relay on this machine)</td><td>25</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				A port in the URL (<code>host:2525</code>) overrides the default. When the URL has a user, the
				client signs in with <code>AUTH PLAIN</code>; write any special characters in the user or the
				password with <code>%</code> escapes, as in any URL. The password goes in clear only when the URL
				says <code>?tls=never</code>.
			</p>
			<p>
				Once the server has started, the plugin connects to the SMTP server (TLS and all), says goodbye,
				and logs whether it answered, under <code>email.smtp</code>. A wrong URL shows on the log then, not
				at the first email. An SMTP server that doesn't answer doesn't stop the app.
			</p>

			<Heading id="mailbox">The dev mailbox</Heading>
			<p>
				A server started with <code>--dev</code> (as <code>rocx dev</code> starts it) answers
				<code>/__rocx/mail</code> before your app does. It lists the email kept instead of sent, newest
				first (when, from, to, subject), with a Clear button, and the list updates as mail arrives. Each
				message has its own page at <code>{"/__rocx/mail/<id>"}</code>: its headers (Cc, Bcc and Reply-To
				too, and your own), its text, its HTML in a sandboxed frame (no script runs in it, and a link opens
				in a new tab), and the whole message as SMTP would have carried it, in which no header names the
				Bcc.
			</p>
			<p>
				The last 100 messages are kept in the server's memory (<a href={Paths.platform_kv}><code>Kv</code></a>),
				with or without a database, so a restart forgets them. <code>/__rocx/mail.json</code> has the same
				messages as JSON, for tests: the <a href={Paths.ecosystem_testing}>testing package</a> reads it to
				find the link in a sign-in email. A server started with <code>--debug</code> and not
				<code>--dev</code> serves only the JSON. Without either flag, these paths are your app's.
			</p>

			<Heading id="inside">How it works</Heading>
			<p>
				The package is Roc on what the server platform gives every plugin. <code>Email.roc</code> writes the
				message: it reads every address first, a subject or a name that isn't plain ASCII is encoded
				(RFC 2047), a body that isn't is sent as Base64, and text with HTML becomes
				<code>multipart/alternative</code>. <code>Smtp.roc</code> is a small SMTP client over the
				platform's <code>Tcp</code>, which has TLS: a <code>RCPT TO</code> for each recipient, then the
				message. Each send opens its own connection. <code>Mailbox.roc</code> is the dev mailbox: paths a plugin answers before the app,
				only under <code>--dev</code> or <code>--debug</code>
				(<a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#plugins"}>plugins</a>), drawn in the
				<a href={Paths.ecosystem_admin}>admin's</a> look.
			</p>
			<p>
				If you don't list the plugin, <code>Email.send!</code> still works: it reads <code>SMTP_URL</code>
				and <code>MAIL_FROM</code> itself on each send. You lose the mailbox's pages, the check of the SMTP
				server, and the refusal to start without one: a send fails instead.
			</p>

			<Heading id="limits">Not yet</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li>No attachments.</li>
				<li>No groups in an address list (<code>{"undisclosed-recipients:;"}</code>), and no comments in an address.</li>
				<li>Mail kept for the mailbox lives in memory only, so a restart forgets it. That is on purpose.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				The package's <code>README.md</code> (<code>packages/email/</code>) has the full reference.
			</p>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
